# Appended to your .gitignore by `factory init`.

# THE FACTORY'S OWN BYTECODE. Every factory script is Python, and running one inside a
# worktree writes `factory/__pycache__/*.pyc` -- inside `factory/`, which is on the
# PROTECTED list. A builder's `git add -A` then sweeps them in and the guard blocks the
# change for touching a protected path, which is exactly right and completely useless:
# the lap dies with five .pyc files named as the violation and the actual work sitting
# beside them, correct and unmergeable.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo

# factory: runtime state, never committed
.factory/runs/
.factory/ledger.jsonl
.factory/locks-runtime/
.factory/findings/
.factory/needs-human.md
.factory/escalations.log
.factory/trigger.json
.factory/deployed.json
.factory/deploy-history.log
.factory/STOP
# The singleton's pid file. Per-machine runtime state: committing it puts one
# machine's pid in another machine's checkout, where it is at best noise and at
# worst a number that happens to belong to something else and wedges the loop.
.factory/loop.pid
.factory/assumptions/
.factory/followups/

# What the agent-driven rungs wrote THIS run. Evidence for one gate, not source.
# Committing them would let a stale result from a build that no longer exists be
# read as the current answer.
harness/.results/
.factory/holdout/.results/

# Secrets. THE PRE-FLIGHT REFUSES TO START while any of these is unignored, and that
# refusal is the point: an empty `git check-ignore` result means the next run that
# commits publishes your key. On a public repo that is publication, not a mistake you
# can take back -- rotating afterwards is the cleanup, not the fix.
#
# Being unable to EDIT a protected file does not stop a broad `git add` from
# committing one that appears for the first time next month, which is why this list
# is separate from the protected list and checked before every committing workflow.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
secrets.json
credentials.json

# graft's local graph cache — regenerable, not committed (run `graft build`).
/graft/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Baseline hygiene (added with the 2026-11 baseline snapshot).
# Everything below is local runtime state, scratch diagnostics, or build output.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Logs and captured process output
logs/
**/logs/
*.stderr.log
*.stdout.log
*.log

# Test / Playwright output
test-results/
**/test-results/
.playwright-mcp/
.playwright/
playwright-report/

# Build + packaging output
web-dist-*.tgz
apps/web/dist-*.tar.gz
apps/web/dist-*.tgz

# Root-level screenshots and captures (*.png is already ignored repo-wide above)
/*.png
/*.jpg
/*.jpeg

# One-off scratch scripts written at the repo root.
# These are anchored with a leading slash on purpose: an unanchored
# "q*.cjs" also matched services/api/query_*.cjs, and opencode still
# hashed those files into its snapshot trees. A file whose blob was
# missing from .git/objects made `git write-tree` fail with
# "invalid object ... for 'scripts/quick_get.cjs'", which broke every
# snapshot the agent took (agent appeared stuck, snapshot never landed).
/*_tmp.mjs
/*_tmp.cjs
/*_tmp.js
/.tmp-*
/.tmp-*.cjs
/pw-*.cjs
/pw-*.js
/q*.cjs
/diag*.bat
/diag*.ps1

# Python virtualenvs (services/api/.venv-ocr is ~13k files of vendored OCR deps)
**/.venv/
**/.venv-*/
**/venv/

# Stray nested copy of the tree (accidental `cp -r` of the repo into itself);
# tracked through the real root paths, never through this duplicate.
/smt-factory-system/

# Vendored third-party checkouts / removed service dirs that are git repos in
# their own right — they cannot be committed as content, and they are not
# submodules of this project.
/tools/VibeVoice/
/integrations/openmes-source.removed/

# Machine/runtime state — never source
*.pid
.codex-*
.runtime/
runtime-logs/
backups/
artifacts/
tmp/

# --- Migrations and seeds are SOURCE, not generated artifacts ----------------
# The blanket `database/` (line 22) and `*.sql` (line 42) rules exist to keep
# dumps, local schema sketches and one-off query files out of git. Applied
# migrations and seeds are the opposite: they ARE the schema, they are
# append-only, and the project cannot be rebuilt without them. Re-include the
# two directories explicitly; everything else under database/ stays ignored.
!database/
database/*
!database/migrations/
!database/migrations/**
!database/seeds/
!database/seeds/**
!database/docker/
!database/docker/**
!database/*.md

